## [STRAPI-CLIENT-1] Strapi Client Overview

### [STRAPI-CLIENT-1.1] Concept and Purpose

The Strapi Client is an official library that simplifies interaction with the Strapi backend:

* Provides a JavaScript/TypeScript interface to access the Strapi REST API
* Facilitates CRUD operations (Create, Read, Update, Delete) on content managed by Strapi
* Replaces the need to manually configure HTTP requests to the Strapi API
* Offers support for advanced features like internationalization, draft/publish, and media uploads

### [STRAPI-CLIENT-1.2] Advantages over Direct HTTP Requests

* **Consistent interface**: Standardized methods for different content types
* **Authentication management**: Simplified API token configuration
* **Parameter handling**: Automatic construction of complex queries
* **Error handling**: Standardized and more informative error response
* **TypeScript support**: Typing for greater safety during development

## [STRAPI-CLIENT-2] Installation and Configuration

### [STRAPI-CLIENT-2.1] Prerequisites

Before using the Strapi Client, make sure that:

* A Strapi project has been created and is running
* You know the Content API URL of your Strapi instance (e.g.: `http://localhost:1337/api`)
* The development environment has access to a package manager (npm, yarn, or pnpm)

### [STRAPI-CLIENT-2.2] Installation

Install the Strapi Client as a dependency using your preferred package manager:

```bash
# Using npm
npm install @strapi/client

# Using yarn
yarn add @strapi/client

# Using pnpm
pnpm add @strapi/client
```

### [STRAPI-CLIENT-2.3] Basic Configuration

To start interacting with the Strapi backend, initialize the client with the base URL of the API:

```javascript
import {
    strapi
} from '@strapi/client';

const client = strapi({
    baseURL: 'http://localhost:1337/api'
});
```

In browser environments, you can include the Strapi Client using a `<script>` tag:

```html
<script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/@strapi/client"></script>

<script>
    const client = strapi.strapi({
        baseURL: 'http://localhost:1337/api'
    });
</script>
```

### [STRAPI-CLIENT-2.4] Configuration Options

The initialization method `strapi()` accepts a configuration object with the following options:

| Parameter   | Type   | Description                                | Required |
|-------------|--------|--------------------------------------------|----------|
| baseURL     | string | Base URL for the Strapi API                | Yes      |
| auth        | string | API token for authentication               | No       |
| prefix      | string | Custom prefix for API routes               | No       |
| axiosOptions| object | Options passed to the Axios instance       | No       |

Example with additional options:

```javascript
const client = strapi({
    baseURL: 'http://localhost:1337/api',
    auth: 'your-api-token-here',
    axiosOptions: {
        timeout: 5000,
        headers: {
            'Custom-Header': 'value'
        }
    }
});
```

## [STRAPI-CLIENT-3] Main Methods

### [STRAPI-CLIENT-3.1] High-Level Methods

The Strapi Client provides the following main methods and properties:

| Method/Property | Description                                                         |
|-----------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------|
| fetch()         | Utility method for generic API requests (similar to native fetch)   |
| collection()    | Manages collection type resources (e.g., blog posts, products)      |
| single()        | Manages single type resources (e.g., homepage settings)             |
| files           | Access to the Media Library to manage files                         |

### [STRAPI-CLIENT-3.2] Generic Fetch Method

The `fetch()` method allows making direct requests to the API, always relative to the base URL provided during client initialization:

```javascript
// Make a GET request to /api/articles
const result = await client.fetch('articles', {
    method: 'GET'
});

// Make a POST request to /api/articles
const newArticle = await client.fetch('articles', {
    method: 'POST',
    data: {
        title: 'New Article',
        content: 'Article content...'
    }
});
```

### [STRAPI-CLIENT-3.3] Common Parameters

These parameters are compatible with all search methods:

| Parameter  | Description                                                         |
|------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------|
| fields     | Specifies which fields should be returned in the response           |
| filters    | Filters results based on conditions                                 |
| populate   | Includes relations, media fields, components, or dynamic zones      |
| sort       | Sorts results by one or more fields                                 |
| pagination | Controls pagination of results                                      |
| locale     | Specifies the locale for internationalized content                  |
| status     | Filters by status (published/draft) when Draft & Publish is enabled |

## [STRAPI-CLIENT-4] Working with Collection Types

### [STRAPI-CLIENT-4.1] Introduction to Collection Types

Collection Types in Strapi are entities that can have multiple entries (e.g., a blog with multiple posts). The Strapi Client provides the `collection()` method to interact with these resources.

Initialization example:

```javascript
const articles = client.collection('articles');
```

### [STRAPI-CLIENT-4.2] Available Methods

| Method                               | Description                                                                     |
|--------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| find(queryParams?)                   | Searches for multiple documents with optional filtering, sorting, or pagination |
| findOne(documentID, queryParams?)    | Retrieves a single document by its ID                                           |
| create(data, queryParams?)           | Creates a new document in the collection                                        |
| update(documentID, data, queryParams?)| Updates an existing document                                                   |
| delete(documentID, queryParams?)     | Deletes an existing document                                                    |

### [STRAPI-CLIENT-4.3] Searching for Multiple Documents

The `find()` method allows searching for a list of documents with various filter parameters:

```javascript
// Search for all articles in English sorted by title
const allArticles = await articles.find({
    locale: 'en',
    sort: 'title',
});

// Search for articles with filters
const filteredArticles = await articles.find({
    filters: {
        title: {
            $contains: 'Tutorial',
        },
        category: {
            name: {
                $eq: 'Technology',
            },
        },
    },
    pagination: {
        page: 1,
        pageSize: 10,
    },
});
```

### [STRAPI-CLIENT-4.4] Searching for a Specific Document

The `findOne()` method retrieves a single document by its ID:

```javascript
// Search for a single article
const singleArticle = await articles.findOne('article-document-id');

// Search for an article with its relations populated
const articleWithRelations = await articles.findOne('article-document-id', {
    populate: {
        author: {
            fields: ['name', 'email'],
        },
        categories: true,
    },
});
```

### [STRAPI-CLIENT-4.5] Creating Documents

The `create()` method allows adding new documents to the collection:

```javascript
// Create a new article
const newArticle = await articles.create({
    title: 'New Article',
    content: 'Article content...',
    categories: {
        connect: ['category-document-id-1', 'category-document-id-2'],
    },
});

// Create an article in a specific locale
const frenchArticle = await articles.create({
    title: 'Nouvel Article',
    content: 'Contenu de l\'article...',
}, {
    locale: 'fr',
});
```

### [STRAPI-CLIENT-4.6] Updating Documents

The `update()` method modifies existing documents:

```javascript
// Update an existing article
const updatedArticle = await articles.update('article-document-id', {
    title: 'Updated Title',
    publishedAt: new Date().toISOString(),
});

// Update an article in a specific locale
const updatedFrenchArticle = await articles.update(
    'article-document-id', {
        title: 'Titre Mis à Jour'
    }, {
        locale: 'fr'
    }
);
```

### [STRAPI-CLIENT-4.7] Deleting Documents

The `delete()` method removes documents from the collection:

```javascript
// Delete an article
await articles.delete('article-document-id');

// Delete an article in a specific locale
await articles.delete('article-document-id', {
    locale: 'fr'
});
```

## [STRAPI-CLIENT-5] Working with Single Types

### [STRAPI-CLIENT-5.1] Introduction to Single Types

Single Types in Strapi represent unique content entries that exist only once (e.g., homepage settings or global site settings). The Strapi Client provides the `single()` method to interact with these resources.

Initialization example:

```javascript
const homepage = client.single('homepage');
```

### [STRAPI-CLIENT-5.2] Available Methods

| Method                    | Description                    |
|---------------------------|--------------------------------|
| find(queryParams?)        | Retrieves the document         |
| update(data, queryParams?)| Updates the document           |
| delete(queryParams?)      | Removes the document           |

### [STRAPI-CLIENT-5.3] Retrieving a Single Type

The `find()` method retrieves the content of the single type:

```javascript
// Retrieve the default homepage content
const defaultHomepage = await homepage.find();

// Retrieve the Spanish version of the homepage
const spanishHomepage = await homepage.find({
    locale: 'es'
});

// Retrieve the homepage with specific fields and relations
const homepageWithRelations = await homepage.find({
    fields: ['title', 'subtitle'],
    populate: {
        seo: true,
        featuredImage: {
            fields: ['url', 'alternativeText'],
        },
    },
});
```

### [STRAPI-CLIENT-5.4] Updating a Single Type

The `update()` method modifies the content of the single type:

```javascript
// Update the homepage
const updatedHomepage = await homepage.update({
    title: 'Updated Homepage Title',
    subtitle: 'New subtitle for the homepage',
});

// Update the Spanish version of the homepage
const updatedSpanishHomepage = await homepage.update({
    title: 'Título Actualizado de la Página Principal',
    subtitle: 'Nuevo subtítulo para la página principal',
}, {
    locale: 'es'
});

// Update the draft version of the homepage
const draftHomepage = await homepage.update({
    title: 'New Homepage (Draft)'
}, {
    status: 'draft'
});
```

### [STRAPI-CLIENT-5.5] Deleting a Single Type

The `delete()` method removes the content of the single type:

```javascript
// Delete the homepage content
await homepage.delete();

// Delete the Spanish version of the homepage
await homepage.delete({
    locale: 'es'
});
```

## [STRAPI-CLIENT-6] Managing Files

### [STRAPI-CLIENT-6.1] Access to the Media Library

The Strapi Client provides access to the Media Library through the `files` property. This allows retrieving and managing file metadata without directly interacting with the REST API.

### [STRAPI-CLIENT-6.2] Available Methods

| Method                   | Description                                                         |
|--------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------|
| find(params?)            | Retrieves a list of file metadata based on optional parameters      |
| findOne(fileId)          | Retrieves the metadata of a single file by its ID                   |
| update(fileId, fileInfo) | Updates metadata of an existing file                                |
| delete(fileId)           | Deletes a file by its ID                                            |

### [STRAPI-CLIENT-6.3] Listing Files

To list all files or filter by certain conditions:

```javascript
// List all files
const allFiles = await client.files.find();

// Filter files by type and name
const imageFiles = await client.files.find({
    filters: {
        mime: {
            $contains: 'image'
        }, // Only image files
        name: {
            $contains: 'avatar'
        }, // Only files with 'avatar' in the name
    },
    sort: ['name:asc'], // Sort by name in ascending order
});

// List files with pagination
const paginatedFiles = await client.files.find({
    pagination: {
        page: 1,
        pageSize: 10,
    },
});
```

### [STRAPI-CLIENT-6.4] Retrieving a Specific File

To fetch the metadata of a specific file:

```javascript
// Fetch file metadata by ID
const file = await client.files.findOne(1);
console.log(file.name); // File name
console.log(file.url); // File URL
console.log(file.mime); // File MIME type
```

### [STRAPI-CLIENT-6.5] Updating File Metadata

To update the metadata of an existing file:

```javascript
// Update file metadata
const updatedFile = await client.files.update(1, {
    name: 'New file name',
    alternativeText: 'Descriptive alternative text for accessibility',
    caption: 'A caption for the file',
});
```

### [STRAPI-CLIENT-6.6] Deleting Files

To delete a file from the media library:

```javascript
// Delete a file by ID
const deletedFile = await client.files.delete(1);
console.log('File successfully deleted');
console.log('Deleted file ID:', deletedFile.id);
console.log('Deleted file name:', deletedFile.name);
```

## [STRAPI-CLIENT-7] Authentication and Security

### [STRAPI-CLIENT-7.1] Authentication Configuration

The Strapi Client supports different authentication strategies to access protected resources in the Strapi backend:

#### Authentication with API Token

```javascript
const client = strapi({
    baseURL: 'http://localhost:1337/api',
    auth: 'your-api-token-here',
});
```

This allows your requests to automatically include the necessary authentication credentials.

### [STRAPI-CLIENT-7.2] Permission Management

When using the Strapi Client, it's important to understand that permissions are controlled on the server side:

* Permissions are defined in the Strapi administration panel
* The Strapi Client only transmits authentication credentials
* If a user doesn't have permission for a resource, the API will return a 403 Forbidden error

### [STRAPI-CLIENT-7.3] Security Best Practices

When implementing the Strapi Client in your applications:

1. **Never expose API tokens in front-end code**:
   * Use environment variables to store tokens
   * In front-end applications, implement a proxy on your server

2. **Implement input validation**:
   * Validate data before sending it to the API
   * Properly handle errors returned by Strapi

3. **Use tokens with minimal permissions**:
   * Create API tokens with only the necessary permissions
   * Limit the scope of tokens to specific APIs when possible

## [STRAPI-CLIENT-8] Advanced Usage Examples

### [STRAPI-CLIENT-8.1] Working with Internationalization (i18n)

When the internationalization plugin is enabled, you can access and manage different localized versions:

```javascript
// Search for articles in different locales
const englishArticles = await articles.find({
    locale: 'en'
});
const frenchArticles = await articles.find({
    locale: 'fr'
});

// Search for all localizations of a specific article
const allLocalesArticle = await articles.find({
    filters: {
        documentId: {
            $eq: 'article-document-id'
        },
    },
    locale: 'all',
});

// Create an article in a specific locale
const spanishArticle = await articles.create({
    title: 'Nuevo Artículo',
    content: 'Contenido del artículo...',
}, {
    locale: 'es'
});

// Update an article in multiple locales
await articles.update('article-document-id', {
    title: 'Updated Title'
}, {
    locale: 'en'
});
await articles.update('article-document-id', {
    title: 'Titre Mis à Jour'
}, {
    locale: 'fr'
});
```

### [STRAPI-CLIENT-8.2] Managing Draft and Published Versions

When the Draft & Publish feature is enabled, you can work with different publication states:

```javascript
// Search for only published articles (default behavior)
const publishedArticles = await articles.find();

// Search for only drafts
const draftArticles = await articles.find({
    status: 'draft'
});

// Create an article as draft
const draftArticle = await articles.create({
    title: 'Draft Article',
    content: 'Draft content...',
}, {
    status: 'draft'
});

// Publish a draft (setting publishedAt to the current date)
await articles.update('draft-article-id', {
    publishedAt: new Date().toISOString(),
});

// Convert a published article to draft
await articles.update('published-article-id', {
    publishedAt: null,
});
```

### [STRAPI-CLIENT-8.3] Managing Relations

The Strapi Client allows managing relations between different content types:

```javascript
// Create an article with related categories
const articleWithCategories = await articles.create({
    title: 'Article with Categories',
    content: 'Article content...',
    categories: {
        connect: ['category-id-1', 'category-id-2'],
    },
});

// Update existing relations
await articles.update('article-id', {
    categories: {
        connect: ['new-category-id'], // Add a new category
        disconnect: ['old-category-id'], // Remove an existing category
    },
});

// Replace all relations
await articles.update('article-id', {
    categories: {
        set: ['category-id-1', 'category-id-3'], // Replaces all existing categories
    },
});
```

### [STRAPI-CLIENT-8.4] Combining Complex Parameters

The Strapi Client allows combining multiple parameters for advanced queries:

```javascript
// Advanced search with multiple parameters
const result = await articles.find({
    filters: {
        $or: [{
                title: {
                    $contains: 'Strapi'
                }
            },
            {
                content: {
                    $contains: 'Strapi'
                }
            },
        ],
        publishedAt: {
            $gte: '2023-01-01',
        },
        category: {
            name: {
                $eq: 'Technology'
            },
        },
    },
    sort: ['publishedAt:desc'],
    populate: {
        author: {
            fields: ['name', 'avatar'],
        },
        categories: {
            fields: ['name', 'slug'],
        },
    },
    pagination: {
        page: 1,
        pageSize: 10,
    },
    locale: 'en',
});
```

### [STRAPI-CLIENT-8.5] Error Handling

Implement proper error handling when using the Strapi Client:

```javascript
try {
    const articles = client.collection('articles');
    const result = await articles.find();
    // Process result
} catch (error) {
    if (error.response) {
        // The server responded with an error status
        console.error('Response error:', error.response.status, error.response.data);

        // Specific handling based on error code
        if (error.response.status === 401) {
            // Authentication problem
            console.error('Authentication failed. Check your API token.');
        } else if (error.response.status === 403) {
            // Permission problem
            console.error('No permission to access this resource.');
        } else if (error.response.status === 404) {
            // Resource not found
            console.error('The requested resource does not exist.');
        }
    } else if (error.request) {
        // The request was made but no response was received
        console.error('No response from server:', error.request);
    } else {
        // Error during request setup
        console.error('Error configuring request:', error.message);
    }
}
```
